<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zaporozhian Cossacks</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th–18th centuries</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Zaporozhians</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zaporozhian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> were the Cossacks of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>, organised from the 16th century around the fortified Zaporozhian Sich beyond the river's rapids. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohdan-khmelnytsky">Bohdan Khmelnytsky</a> they led the rising of 1648 against Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> that created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossack-hetmanate">Cossack Hetmanate</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> destroyed the Sich in 1775, and many former Zaporozhians were later resettled as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> Cossack Host in the Kuban.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zaporozhian-cossacks.jpg" alt="Zaporozhian Cossacks" width="320" height="189" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ilya Repin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ilja_Jefimowitsch_Repin_-_Reply_of_the_Zaporozhian_Cossacks_-_Yorck.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name comes from Zaporizhzhia, &quot;the land beyond the rapids&quot;, where the fortress of the Zaporozhians, the Sich, stood. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> is traced to a Turkic word meaning a free man, and the Cossacks were predominantly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruthenians">Ruthenians</a> who included other groups. It is not clear when the first Cossack communities formed on the Lower Dnieper; similar people lived on the steppe from the 12th century, and the part played by nomads such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> in their origins is disputed. Many were escaped serfs who preferred the dangerous freedom of the steppe to life under Polish or Polonised aristocrats, and newcomers had to accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodoxy">Eastern Orthodoxy</a>. In the 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmytro-vyshnevetsky">Dmytro Vyshnevetsky</a>, a Ruthenian noble, united these groups into a strong military organization.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 16th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> regarded the Zaporozhians as its subjects, though tentatively, and registered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> were part of its army until 1699. From the later 16th century their raids on Ottoman territory, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> raids answered, strained relations between the Commonwealth and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> and helped lead to wars such as the Battle of Cecora in 1620. After the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereiaslav">Pereyaslav</a> in 1654 the Host became a suzerainty under the protection of the tsar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, though it long kept near-complete autonomy.</p><p>By the late 18th century much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> officer class in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> had been absorbed into the Russian nobility, and many rank-and-file Cossacks had been reduced to peasant status. The Sich sheltered runaway serfs, which angered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a>. Her council decided on 7 May 1775 to disband it. General Peter Tekeli surrounded the Sich with artillery and infantry on 5 June, and the Cossacks were overwhelmed. In 1784 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grigory-potemkin">Grigory Potemkin</a> formed the Host of the Loyal Zaporozhians between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-bug">Southern Bug</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Host was governed by the unwritten Cossack Code. It had 38 kurins and five to eight palankas, and all officers were elected by the General Military Council for a year on 1 January. Its leader never officially held the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetman">hetman</a>, though Cossack leaders were unofficially called so. The highest body was the Sich Rada, the council that decided matters of foreign and internal policy, elections, the division of land and the punishment of grave crimes, which is why some sources call the Sich a Cossack republic. Hetmans and kish otamans carried the bulawa, or mace; the one carried by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohdan-khmelnytsky">Bohdan Khmelnytsky</a> from 1648 was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, covered in gold and set with pearls and gems.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zaporozhian Cossacks” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Muscovy responded by building fortified lines, the Great Abatis Belt and later the Belgorod Line, while the <b>Zaporozhian Cossacks</b> grew partly as a frontier society facing the Tatars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Thirteen Years’ War of 1654–1667, after the Pereiaslav agreement with the <b>Ukrainian Cossacks</b>, Russia regained Smolensk and won Kyiv and left-bank Ukraine by the Truce of Andrusovo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tsardom-of-russia">Tsardom of Russia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1787, on the eve of war with the Ottoman Empire, Prince Grigory Potemkin gathered former <b>Zaporozhian Cossacks</b> into the Host of Loyal Zaporozhians.</blockquote><a href="/state/black-sea-cossack-host">Black Sea Cossack Host</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kuban Cossack Host was formed in 1860 by merging the Black Sea Cossack Host, made up of former <b>Zaporozhian Cossacks</b> resettled on the Kuban in 1792, with part of the Caucasus Line Cossack Host.</blockquote><a href="/state/kuban-cossack-host">Kuban Cossack Host</a></li></ul></section>
